properties.statement:
READ FROM SOURCE 2026-08-10 while starting BL-198: reflow2's schema YAML declares 81 `default:` values across 39 node types, and **dynograph-core APPLIES THEM AT WRITE TIME**. `dynograph-core/src/schema.rs:507`, in the validation path every upsert goes through:

    for (prop_name, prop_def) in &node_def.properties {
        if !properties.contains_key(prop_name) && let Some(default) = &prop_def.default {
            properties.insert(prop_name.clone(), default.clone());
        }

So a caller who says nothing gets the default STORED. That is why `add_capability` never mentions `tier` and every Capability nonetheless carries `tier: operational`, and why 3,207 stored values across 1,178 of 1,980 nodes — 59% of this design — equal a schema-declared default.

⚠️ THIS NODE REPLACES A WRONG ONE I RECORDED TEN MINUTES EARLIER, `fact:the-schema-declares-81-defaults-and-nothing-reads-them`, which claimed the declarations were parsed by nobody. DELETED RATHER THAN RETIRED, because it was never true (retire-from-design's one question). The error: I grepped dynograph's schema.rs for `default`, saw a screenful of `#[serde(default)]` attributes in the head of the output, and concluded the field did not exist. `pub default: Option<Value>` is on line 127 of that same file. A confident answer from an incomplete look — the session's own recurring failure, arrived at for the fourth time.

⭐ THE CORRECTION IMPROVES THE PLAN RATHER THAN MERELY FIXING IT. `default:` in the YAML is not documentation, it is an INSTRUCTION TO INJECT. So BL-198's "stop storing a value nobody chose" has a route that needs no code change and crosses no repo boundary: **REMOVE THE `default:` KEY from the properties where injection is wrong.** dynograph then injects nothing, the property is genuinely absent, and absence honestly means nobody said.

THE CONSEQUENCE THAT MAKES THIS CHEAP: the change is FORWARD-ONLY. Existing nodes keep the values already stored, so the committed export does not churn and `compare_designs` sees nothing — the phantom-diff cost BL-198 warned about, and that step 2 of `dec:fix-the-comparison-before-pointing-it-earlier` exists to disclose, DOES NOT ARISE for this route at all. Removing a `default:` costs one YAML line per property and no migration.

WHAT STILL NEEDS THE OWNER'S JUDGEMENT, per property: whether the value means "nobody said" or is a MEANINGFUL INITIAL STATE. `Requirement.status = proposed` is the latter — the whole certainty doctrine reads it (`dec:certainty-derived`, graph_report's certainty line, detect_gaps, loop_status, what_next), and removing its default would break derivation rather than clean it. The candidates that carry no information are the descriptive enums nobody chooses: Requirement.kind/concern/lineage/designation, Capability.tier, Component.kind, Fragment.fragment_type.

AND A SECOND-ORDER QUESTION THIS OPENS: with the `default:` removed, where does the CONVENTIONAL value get stated for a reader who wants to know what absence implies? It cannot stay in the key dynograph consumes. Either reflow2 keeps its own table, or the value stops being conventional at all — which for a descriptive enum nobody chooses is arguably the honest answer.
